Exactly. The absence of vice is not the presence of virtue.

“Not being bad” is mostly a negative constraint: don’t lie, don’t steal, don’t hurt people, don’t betray. You can satisfy all of that by doing almost nothing.

Being good is generative. It requires judgment and action: telling the truth when it costs you, helping when you could walk away, protecting someone, repairing what you damaged, giving something up for another person, creating conditions that are better because you were there.

So there are really three states, not two:

harmful → passive/neutral → beneficial

And that middle state matters. Someone can be harmless without being kind, innocent without being courageous, polite without being compassionate, and lawful without being just.

That also explains why goodness can sometimes look superficially “bad.” Setting a boundary, confronting somebody, disciplining a child, telling an unpleasant truth, cutting something back so it grows better—those can involve discomfort or harm at the local scale while serving something valuable at the larger scale.

The harder moral question therefore isn’t merely “Did I do anything wrong?”

It’s also “What did the situation ask of me that I chose not to do?”

Goodness has a verb in it.